<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as The Kirk, the Kirk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, known as the Kirk, is Scotland's national church, Presbyterian in government and Reformed in doctrine. It emerged from the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of 1560 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a> and, after long conflict with the Stuart kings over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, was confirmed as Presbyterian in 1690. Its position was guaranteed by the Treaty of Union in 1707, and it is governed by a General Assembly rather than bishops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland.jpg" alt="Church of Scotland" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaihsu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NecTamenConsumebatur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to traditional Scottish historiography, the first Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were converted by Saint Ninian around 400, and Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> founded a mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> about two centuries later. In 1192 the papal bull Cum universi separated the Scottish church from the Archbishopric of York, making it an independent national church with no higher authority than the Pope. The Church of Scotland in its present form traces its origins to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of 1560, when many in the church in Scotland broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in a Protestant reform led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a>, who had been exposed to the principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> while living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An assembly of nobles, lairds, burgesses and some churchmen, claiming in defiance of the queen to be a Scottish Parliament, abolished papal jurisdiction and approved the Scots Confession in 1560. Mary I, a Catholic, refused to ratify the settlement, and the acts were finally approved in 1572 by the young James VI. The Concordat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leith">Leith</a> let the crown appoint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and in response a Presbyterian party emerged under Andrew Melville, author of the Second Book of Discipline.</p><p>Charles I and William Laud tried to bring Scottish worship closer to English practice, and the Scottish Prayer Book of 1637 caused rioting at St Giles Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-covenant">National Covenant</a> was signed in early 1638, and in November the General Assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> declared the Prayer Book unlawful and abolished the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a> later made common cause with the English parliamentarians, and the Westminster Confession of Faith, agreed by both, remains the church's subordinate standard. Episcopacy returned after the Restoration, but in 1690, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>, the Scots Parliament guaranteed Presbyterian governance of the church. Later disputes over patronage led to the secession of 1733 and the Disruption of 1843, when a large part of the church formed the Free Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The church is Presbyterian in polity and Reformed in theology. It has no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and no single head, since it holds that role to belong to God. Elders and ministers, called presbyters, sit in a series of courts: each congregation is led by a Session, which answers to a regional presbytery, and the supreme body is the General Assembly, which meets each May in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and is chaired by a Moderator elected for a year. It celebrates two sacraments, baptism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Lord's Supper</a>. Since 1968 all ministries and offices have been open to women and men equally, and Alison Elliot became the first woman Moderator in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Church of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in 1706, and the Scottish Parliament ratified it in January 1707 amid popular protest, persuaded by access to English and colonial markets, by the ‘Equivalent’, a payment that partly compensated investors in the failed Darien scheme, and by guarantees for the Presbyterian <b>Church of Scotland</b> and Scots law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Reformation of 1560 <b>the Kirk</b> aimed at a school in every parish, a goal written into law by an act of 1696.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Episcopalians would keep bishops that supported reform, presbyterians wanted a system of elders on the model of the <b>Church of Scotland</b>, and Congregationalists or Independents held that each local church of visible saints was complete within itself.</blockquote><a href="/movement/puritans">Puritans · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The English Parliament and the Westminster Assembly accepted the Solemn League and Covenant with the Scottish Covenanters, already approved by the <b>Church of Scotland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
